$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 10/Prevalence effects

::EPI-W10-042::Holding sensitivity and specificity constant, what usually happens to PPV as disease prevalence rises? {
=PPV rises#Correct.
~PPV falls#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~PPV is always unchanged#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~Sensitivity becomes zero#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
}

::EPI-W10-043::The same test is moved from a high-prevalence specialist clinic to a low-prevalence screening population in scenario 1. What is the most likely change, all else equal? {
=PPV decreases and NPV increases#Correct. Predictive values depend on pre-test prevalence.
~PPV increases and NPV decreases#Sensitivity and specificity may remain stable while predictive values change.
~Sensitivity necessarily falls to zero#Sensitivity and specificity may remain stable while predictive values change.
~Specificity necessarily becomes 100%#Sensitivity and specificity may remain stable while predictive values change.
}

