$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 10/Testing algorithms

::EPI-W10-064::What is the usual effect of requiring two tests to be positive in series? {
=Overall specificity tends to increase while sensitivity can decrease#Correct.
~Overall sensitivity always becomes 100%#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~Both sensitivity and specificity become zero#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~Prevalence doubles#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
}

::EPI-W10-065::If either of two screening tests being positive is enough to classify a person positive, what broad strategy is this? {
=Parallel testing#Correct.
~Serial testing#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Case-control sampling#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Blinded verification#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
}

::EPI-W10-066::In a laboratory evaluation, if either of two screening tests being positive is enough to classify a person positive, what broad strategy is this? {
=Parallel testing#Correct.
~Serial testing#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Case-control sampling#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Blinded verification#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
}

::EPI-W10-067::In a point-of-care test study, if either of two screening tests being positive is enough to classify a person positive, what broad strategy is this? {
=Parallel testing#Correct.
~Serial testing#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Case-control sampling#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
~Blinded verification#Parallel algorithms tend to prioritise sensitivity.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 10/Bias and interpretation

::EPI-W10-068::Why does high agreement between two tests not prove diagnostic accuracy? {
=Both tests could agree and still be wrong relative to true disease status#Correct.
~Agreement is mathematically impossible#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~Accuracy never needs a reference standard#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
~Specificity equals agreement#Review which population is in the denominator and what the test comparison represents.
}

::EPI-W10-069::Only patients with a positive index test receive the reference standard. What is the key diagnostic-study concern? {
=Verification bias#Correct.
~Effect modification#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
~Ecological fallacy#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
~Randomisation failure#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
}

::EPI-W10-070::In a fever-screening study, only patients with a positive index test receive the reference standard. What is the key diagnostic-study concern? {
=Verification bias#Correct.
~Effect modification#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
~Ecological fallacy#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
~Randomisation failure#Verification depends on the index test result, distorting observed accuracy.
}
